course: everyday
lesson: 110
title: Windows, balconies and furniture
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Many injuries at home come from falls and from heavy things falling on children, so windows, balconies and furniture need care.
explain: A tall bookshelf or chest of drawers can tip over when a child climbs it or pulls out its drawers, which is why grown-ups fix tall furniture to the wall.
explain: Height makes things dangerous: a fall from a window or a top bunk hurts badly, and even a small object dropped from a balcony can seriously hurt someone below.
explain: For example, a girl in a New York apartment never sits on the windowsill and keeps her desk away from the window, so she cannot climb up and fall.
fact: Never lean out of an upstairs window or sit on a windowsill, and keep furniture away from windows.
fact: Falls from windows badly hurt or kill children every year.
fact: Blind cords should be tied up high or wound on a cleat (a small hook fixed high on the wall), because a young child can get one around the neck and strangle.
fact: Opening a window for a short time in winter changes the air without wasting much heat.
fact: Never throw or drop anything from a balcony, because a falling object can seriously hurt someone below.
fact: Never stand on a chair or climb anything on a balcony, because you could fall over the railing.
fact: Never climb furniture, because it can tip over and crush a child.
fact: Sit with all four chair legs on the floor, because a tipped chair slips and you hit your head.
fact: Jump only where it is allowed, never near windows or sharp corners.
fact: Only one person goes on the top bunk, using the ladder, with no play on top, and children under six should not sleep there.
fact: Toddlers fall against the sharp corners of tables, so they need watching and grown-ups can fit corner guards.
fact: Pull drawers gently and only partly, because a heavy drawer can fall out and many open drawers can tip the chest.
fact: Keep fingers clear of the folding parts of recliners and folding chairs, because they pinch and crush.
exercise: You want to lean out of your bedroom window upstairs to see a parade. What do you do? | Look from inside without leaning out | Lean out but hold on tight with one hand | Sit on the windowsill to see better | Stand on a chair by the window
exercise: Why should furniture be kept away from windows? | Children can climb it and fall out | Furniture blocks too much sunlight | The window glass can scratch it | Furniture makes the room too cold
exercise: The blind cord hangs loose near your baby brother's cot. What should be done? | Tie it up high or ask a grown-up to fit a cleat | Leave it, babies are far too small to reach any cords | Tie it to the cot to keep it tidy | Cut a knot in it to make it shorter
exercise: It is winter and the heating is on, but the room is stuffy. What is sensible? | Open the window for a short while, then close it | Open the window and leave it open all day | Never open a window in winter, even when the air is stale | Turn the heating off for the whole day
exercise: You are on a balcony and want to drop a ball to your friend below. What is right? | Never drop anything; carry it down instead | Drop it if you shout first | Drop it only if it is a small, soft ball like a tennis ball | Throw it gently towards the grass
exercise: Your toy is on top of the bookshelf. How do you get it? | Ask a grown-up to get it for you | Climb up the shelves like a ladder | Stand on the bottom drawer | Pull the bookshelf towards you
exercise: Why do grown-ups fix tall furniture to the wall? | So it cannot tip over and crush a child | So it looks much neater and tidier in the room | So it does not get dusty behind | So nobody can move it to clean
exercise: You lean back on two legs of your chair in class. What can happen? | The chair slips and you hit your head | The chair becomes more comfortable to sit on for long | You can see the board better | Nothing, chairs are made for it
exercise: You want to jump on the sofa. Where is it unsafe? | Near the window and sharp corners | In the middle of a trampoline | On a soft mat in a gym | On the soft grass in the garden, away from everything
exercise: You and your friend want to play on the top bunk together. What is the rule? | Only one person on top, using the ladder, no play | Two people is fine as long as you are both really careful up there | Climbing up the side is fine | Jumping down is fine if you land on pillows
exercise: Your four-year-old sister wants to sleep on the top bunk. What is right? | Children under six should not sleep on top | She can sleep there if she promises to be very careful | She can if she has a pillow on the floor | She can if her big sister is below
exercise: Your toddler cousin is running near a glass coffee table. What do you do? | Watch him closely, as the corners are at head height | Let him run, toddlers do not fall | Put more things on the table | Sit on the table yourself so that you hide the sharp corner
exercise: You pull a heavy drawer all the way out. What could happen? | It falls on your feet | It goes back in by itself | Nothing, drawers cannot fall out | It makes the chest heavier and steadier
exercise: Why should you not open all the drawers of a chest at once? | The chest can tip forward | The drawers will get stuck | The clothes will get creased | It lets dust into the drawers
exercise: You are folding up a camping chair. What do you watch out for? | Keep fingers clear of the folding parts | Fold it as fast as you can so it snaps shut | Hold it by the hinges | Sit on it while folding it
test: Jacob in Sydney wants to climb the chest of drawers to reach a kite. What is the danger? | It can tip over and crush him | The drawers will get dirty from his shoes | The kite will fall and break | He might wake the baby
test: Why can even a small coin dropped from a high balcony be dangerous? | It falls fast and can hurt someone below | Coins are always very heavy for their size | It will break the balcony | It will get lost and nobody can find it
test: A baby's cot is under a window with a long blind cord. What is the safest change? | Move the cot and tie the cord up high | Open the window to let the cord swing | Hang more toys from the cord | Give the baby the cord to play with
test: Which is true about bunk beds? | Falls from top bunks are a common injury | Top bunks are safe for children of any age | Top bunks are good for wrestling | You should jump down to save time
test: Why do many families fit corner guards to tables when they have a toddler? | Toddlers fall against sharp corners at head height | Corner guards make the tables last much longer | Toddlers like to chew the guards | The tables look nicer with them
test: Leo keeps rocking on two chair legs at dinner. What would you tell him? | Keep all four legs down or you could fall and hit your head | Rocking on two legs makes your back stronger and straighter | Only rock when the floor is wet | Rocking on chairs is good exercise
test: Why open a window only briefly on a cold day? | It changes the air without wasting much heat | Cold air is bad for you at any time of the year | The window will freeze open | Fresh air is only useful in summer
test: A grandad's recliner chair closes with a snap. What should a child keep clear? | Fingers, away from the folding parts | Feet, away from the cushions | Elbows, away from the armrests on each side | Hair, away from the back
test: Which is the safest place for a desk in an upstairs bedroom? | Away from the window | Right under the open window | On the balcony outside | Against the windowsill
test: can i sit on the windowsill upstairs, nice view | No, you could fall out, sit away from the window | Yes, if the window is shut it is totally fine | Yes, if you hold the curtain | Yes, if you're over eight
test: bored, gonna drop my water balloon off the balcony on ppl lol | No, falling things can badly hurt someone below | Fine if it's only water | Fine if you shout first | Fine if it's from a low balcony, it's only water
test: whats the big deal climbing the bookcase | It can tip over and crush you | Books might get a bit dusty | It only matters if you're heavy | Bookcases are fixed so it's fine
test: can my 4 yo cousin sleep on the top bunk | No, children under six shouldn't sleep up top | Yes, if he's not scared | Yes, with a pillow on the floor | Yes, if two kids sleep up there together to keep him safe
explain: A little boy in New York climbed onto a chair by an open window and leaned on the screen; it gave way and he fell two floors and was badly hurt.
explain: A girl in Texas climbed a tall bookcase to reach a toy; it tipped over onto her, and she was trapped under it until her dad lifted it off.
fact: Window screens keep insects out but cannot hold the weight of a child.
fact: Tall furniture and TVs that are not fixed to the wall can tip over and crush small children.
test: lean on the window screen to look out?? | No, screens can't hold you and you can fall | Yes, screens are really strong, it's fine | Yes, if you're light | Only on the ground floor
test: toy on top of the bookcase, gonna climb up | Ask an adult, it can tip over on you | Climb up fast so it doesn't have time to tip | Pull the shelves down | Stand on the bottom shelf only
test: balcony, can i stand on the chair to see better | No, you could fall over the railing | Yes, if you hold on with one hand | Yes, it's only a few floors | Only if it's windy
test: did u hear a little kid leaned on a window screen n fell out, the screen just popped off, mum's friend saw the ambulance. what's the real lesson | Screens keep bugs out but cannot hold a child, so never lean on them | Window screens are strong enough to lean on if you are only a small child | Only old screens are dangerous to lean on | Leaning is fine if the window is half open
test: Ruby's telling everyone a girl in Texas got crushed by a bookcase n was stuck under it for a week. what's probably exaggerated | A week; tall furniture can tip and must be fixed to the wall | That a bookcase can tip over on a child | That she climbed it to reach a toy | Nothing, because bookcases are far too heavy ever to tip over on a child
test: Dan dropped a water balloon off the balcony onto some old man, everyone thinks it's hilarious n is sharing the vid. should u share it | No, dropping things can hurt people below, so do not share it | Yes, because water balloons can never hurt anyone | Yes, but crop the old man out of the video first so nobody knows who he is | Yes, so everyone sees how funny Dan is
exam: Identify the principal reason for which tall furniture is fixed to the wall. | It may otherwise tip over and crush a child | It is otherwise liable to be stolen by intruders | It may otherwise be scratched by the floor | It is required solely for decoration
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dropping of an object from a balcony? | Even a small object may seriously injure someone below | It is harmless provided the object is light | It is harmless provided nobody is watching | It is dangerous only from the tenth floor or higher
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of a top bunk. | One person, using the ladder, no play; not under six years | Any number of children, provided they sit still | Children under six may sleep there if they are tall enough | Play is permitted provided the ladder is removed
exam: Pursuant to the hanging of blind cords, ascertain the precaution required in respect of young children. | Tie them up high or wind them on a cleat | Let them hang loose to the floor in reach | Tie them into a loop near the windowsill | Remove the blind and leave the cord in place
